::ALG2_U02_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial height model has H(0) = 6. What does 6 represent? {
=the initial height #Correct. H(0) is the output at time 0, so it represents the starting height.
~the landing time #Landing time is usually a zero.
~the maximum height #A maximum is a vertex or peak value.
~the degree #Function output does not identify degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If P(t) models profit and P(t) = 0, what does t represent? {
~maximum profit #Maximum profit occurs at a peak.
=a break-even time #Correct. Profit equal to zero means revenue and cost are equal.
~initial profit #Initial profit is P(0).
~the degree of the model #The input t does not identify degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A ball height model has zeros at t = 0 and t = 5. What is a reasonable domain? {
~all real numbers #Negative time and time after landing may not fit.
~t < 0 #Negative time is not meaningful.
=0 <= t <= 5 #Correct. The model is physically meaningful from launch until landing.
~t >= 5 only #That begins at landing.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A revenue model reaches a maximum at x = 40. What does x = 40 represent? {
~the greatest revenue amount #The y-coordinate gives the maximum revenue.
~a zero of the model #A zero is where output is 0.
~the initial revenue #Initial revenue is the output when x = 0.
=the input that gives greatest revenue #Correct. The x-coordinate of the maximum gives the input where the peak occurs.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why might a negative solution be rejected in a length model? {
=lengths cannot be negative #Correct. A physical length must be zero or positive.
~negative numbers are never solutions #Negative numbers can solve equations.
~all models use positive coefficients #Coefficient signs do not decide valid context values.
~it must be the y-intercept #A rejected solution is not necessarily an intercept.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If C(x) models cost, what does C(0) usually represent? {
~number of units sold #Units are usually the input x.
=fixed or starting cost #Correct. The output at x = 0 describes the cost before any units are produced.
~profit at break-even #Break-even involves profit or revenue equaling cost.
~the largest possible cost #C(0) is not necessarily a maximum.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial model has an x-intercept at 12 months. What could that mean? {
~the initial amount is 12 #Initial amount is the output at input 0.
~the maximum amount is 12 #A maximum is a peak output.
=the modeled quantity is 0 at 12 months #Correct. An x-intercept occurs where the output equals 0.
~the degree is 12 #An intercept location does not identify degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which value should be used to interpret the starting amount in A(t)? {
~A(1) #This is the amount after one time unit.
~t = 0 only #The full output A(0) gives the amount.
~the leading coefficient only #The leading coefficient does not directly give the starting amount.
=A(0) #Correct. The starting amount is the output when the input time is 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model for water volume has V(3) = 0. What does this likely mean? {
=the container is empty at time 3 #Correct. A zero output means the modeled volume is 0.
~the container starts with 3 units #Starting volume would be V(0).
~the maximum volume is 3 #A maximum would be a peak output.
~the model has degree 3 #The input value does not identify degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If a model output is negative for a population, how should it be treated in context? {
~as the exact population #Negative population is not physically meaningful.
=as not meaningful for population #Correct. A real population count cannot be negative.
~as the degree of the polynomial #Output sign does not identify degree.
~as a required maximum #Negative output is not automatically a maximum.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does the y-intercept of a polynomial model often represent? {
~final value always #Final value depends on the context and domain.
~a horizontal asymptote #Polynomial functions do not have horizontal asymptotes here.
=initial value #Correct. The y-intercept occurs when the input is 0.
~the largest zero #The y-intercept is an output, not an x-zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial area model gives A(x) = 0 at x = 4. What does x = 4 likely represent? {
~the maximum area #A zero is not a maximum area.
~the starting area #Starting area is A(0).
~the leading coefficient #The input x = 4 is not a coefficient.
=a boundary value where area becomes zero #Correct. Zeros can mark endpoints or nonphysical boundaries in context.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why is context important when solving polynomial models? {
=some algebraic solutions may not make sense physically #Correct. Context determines which roots or intervals are meaningful.
~it changes the rules of factoring #Factoring rules stay the same.
~it removes the need to check units #Units are part of contextual interpretation.
~it makes every solution positive #Context may reject solutions, but not force all positive.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If a height model opens downward, what key feature often gives maximum height? {
~the smallest zero #A zero gives ground contact, not maximum height.
=the vertex #Correct. The peak of a downward-opening model occurs at the vertex.
~the y-intercept always #Initial height is not always the maximum.
~the leading coefficient alone #It indicates direction, not exact maximum.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial model is valid only for 0 <= t <= 10. Which input should be rejected? {
~t = 0 #The domain includes 0.
~t = 5 #The domain includes values between 0 and 10.
=t = 12 #Correct. This input is outside the stated domain.
~t = 10 #The domain includes 10.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If R(x) - C(x) = 0 in a business model, what does that represent? {
~maximum loss #A zero difference is not a maximum loss.
~initial cost #Initial cost is C(0).
~domain restriction #The equation describes equality, not domain.
=break-even #Correct. Revenue minus cost equal to 0 means profit is 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does an increasing interval tell you in a model? {
=where the output is rising #Correct. An increasing interval shows input values where the modeled quantity grows.
~where the output equals 0 #Zeros describe where output is 0.
~where input is not allowed #Domain restrictions describe allowed inputs.
~where the model must be linear #A polynomial can increase without being linear.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial temperature model has a local minimum. What does that feature represent? {
~the initial temperature always #Initial temperature is T(0).
=a low point over the modeled interval #Correct. A local minimum is a point where nearby outputs are higher.
~a zero always #A minimum may or may not be output 0.
~the degree of the polynomial #A feature point does not identify degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which interpretation matches f(2) = 15 in context? {
~when the output is 2, the input is 15 #That reverses input and output.
~the model has two zeros and degree 15 #Function notation does not state zeros or degree.
=when the input is 2, the output is 15 #Correct. Function notation pairs an input with its output.
~15 is always the maximum #One output value is not necessarily a maximum.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If a polynomial model has zeros at 1 and 9, what interval might represent positive output between zeros? {
~x < 1 or x > 9 #That describes outside the zeros.
~x = 1 and x = 9 only #At the zeros, output is 0.
~all real numbers #Zeros divide intervals where sign may change.
=1 < x < 9 #Correct. If the graph is above the x-axis between zeros, that interval gives positive output.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why should units be included when interpreting a model answer? {
=units explain what the number measures #Correct. A value without units can be mathematically correct but unclear in context.
~units change the algebraic solution #Units do not change the arithmetic result.
~units replace the need for a graph #Graphs and units answer different parts.
~units identify the leading coefficient #Units do not directly identify it.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model predicts maximum height 64 at t = 2. What is 64? {
~the time of maximum height #That is t = 2.
=the maximum output #Correct. The y-value at the peak is the maximum height.
~a zero of the model #A zero would have output 0.
~the domain endpoint #64 is an output value.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which question is answered by finding x-intercepts in a context model? {
~what the initial value is #Initial value is found at x = 0.
~what the leading coefficient is #Leading coefficient comes from the equation.
=when the output is zero #Correct. x-intercepts occur where the function value is 0.
~whether the graph is continuous #Intercepts do not answer that directly.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If a model gives two real zeros but only one is in the domain, what should happen? {
~use both zeros automatically #A solution outside the domain should be rejected.
~reject the whole model #One invalid solution does not invalidate the entire model.
~change the equation #The equation is not changed by context.
=use only the zero in the domain #Correct. Context restricts which algebraic solutions are meaningful.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only application; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does a negative leading coefficient often suggest for the right end of an odd-degree model? {
=the output eventually decreases #Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient falls to the right.
~the output eventually increases #That would match a positive odd-degree leading coefficient.
~the model has no zeros #End behavior does not determine whether zeros exist.
~the initial value is negative #Initial value depends on f(0).
}

